    "id": 62,
    "title": "How would you troubleshoot a Spring Boot application that fails during startup?",
    "category": "4. Spring Boot",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "Startup failures are usually related to Dependency Injection (missing beans) or Configuration (bad URLs). Steps to troubleshoot:\n1. **Read the Stack Trace:** Spring Boot prints a very helpful `Description:` and `Action:` section at the bottom of the stack trace.\n2. **Check Port Conflicts:** A `BindException` means port 8080 is already in use by another app.\n3. **Check Bean Ambiguity:** A `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` means you need a `@Qualifier`.\n4. **Check Circular Dependencies:** Bean A needs Bean B, which needs Bean A.\n5. **Enable Debug Logging:** Add `debug=true` or `--debug` to see a massive report of exactly what Auto-Configuration evaluated, what passed, and what failed.",
    "example": "If you forget to add `@Service` to your class, the app will crash with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`. The logs will literally tell you: \"Action: Consider defining a bean of type 'MyService' in your configuration.\"",
