    "title": "What is the N+1 query problem?",
    "category": "7. Spring Data JPA / Hibernate",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "The N+1 problem is a massive performance killer in ORM frameworks caused by improper handling of Lazy loading.\nIt occurs when you query the database to get a list of 'N' parent entities (1 query). Then, you loop through that list in Java, and access a lazy-loaded child collection on each parent. For every iteration, Hibernate fires a *new* query to get that parent's children (N queries). \nTotal queries = 1 (to get parents) + N (to get children).",
    "example": "You have 100 Authors. Query 1: `SELECT * FROM authors`. Then in a loop, you call `author.getBooks()`. Hibernate fires 100 individual queries: `SELECT * FROM books WHERE author_id=?`. A task that should take 1 JOIN query just took 101 queries.",
    "code": "// The setup for disaster\nList<Author> authors = repository.findAll(); // 1 Query\nfor(Author a : authors) {\n    System.out.println(a.getBooks().size()); // N Queries fired here!\n}",
    "tip": "Monitoring SQL logs (e.g., setting `spring.jpa.show-sql=true`) is the easiest way to spot a machine-gun spray of repetitive SQL queries.",
    "mistake": "Ignoring the N+1 problem until production, where a table with 10,000 rows causes the page to take 45 seconds to load.",
    "summary": "A performance flaw where fetching a list of N entities, and then accessing their lazy child associations, results in N+1 total database queries instead of 1."
