    "id": 121,
    "title": "What is the difference between @OneToOne, @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, and @ManyToMany?",
    "category": "8. Advanced JPA / Hibernate",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "They define the multiplicity of the relationship:\n- **`@OneToOne`:** 1:1. (e.g., `Person` and `Passport`). Mapped via a unique foreign key.\n- **`@ManyToOne`:** N:1. (e.g., `Employees` to `Department`). The most efficient and common. The Employee table holds the `dept_id` foreign key.\n- **`@OneToMany`:** 1:N. (e.g., `Department` to `Employees`). Usually the inverse of `@ManyToOne`. It returns a Collection (List/Set).\n- **`@ManyToMany`:** N:N. (e.g., `Students` and `Courses`). Requires a third, hidden \"Join Table\" in the database (e.g., `student_course`) containing foreign keys pointing to both.",
    "example": "In a blog: `User` (1) to `Profile` (1). `Post` (N) to `User` (1). `User` (1) to `Posts` (N). `Post` (N) to `Tags` (N).",
    "code": "// ManyToMany requires a join table\n@ManyToMany\n@JoinTable(\n  name = \"student_course\", \n  joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = \"student_id\"), \n  inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = \"course_id\"))\nprivate Set<Course> courses;",
