    "category": "8. Advanced JPA / Hibernate",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "Cascading is a convenience feature that propagates database state transitions from a Parent entity down to its Child entities automatically.\nInstead of manually saving a parent, extracting its children, and saving each child one by one, you apply a `CascadeType`. When you perform an action (like `persist` or `delete`) on the parent, Hibernate automatically cascades that exact same action to the children.",
    "example": "If a user deletes their entire account, you don't want to leave orphaned Profile and Address rows in the database. `CascadeType.REMOVE` on the User will automatically delete the children.",
    "code": "@Entity\npublic class Order {\n    // If I save the Order, save all its un-saved Items too.\n    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.PERSIST)\n    private List<OrderItem> items;\n}",
