    "id": 125,
    "title": "What is the difference between save(), saveAndFlush(), and flush()?",
    "category": "8. Advanced JPA / Hibernate",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "- **`save()`:** Adds the entity to the Persistence Context (Hibernate's memory). It does *not* instantly write to the database. Hibernate delays (batches) the actual SQL `INSERT/UPDATE` until the transaction ends to optimize performance.\n- **`flush()`:** Forces Hibernate to instantly synchronize its memory with the database by firing all pending SQL statements immediately, without ending the transaction.\n- **`saveAndFlush()`:** A convenience method in Spring Data JPA that performs a `save()` and immediately calls `flush()`, forcing the SQL to the database immediately.",
