    "id": 134,
    "title": "What is the difference between monolithic architecture and microservices?",
    "category": "9. Microservices",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "- **Monolith:** All UI, business logic, and data access code are packaged together into a single codebase and deployed as a single unit (e.g., one massive `.war` file). They share a single database.\n- **Microservices:** The application is split into multiple independent codebases. Each service is deployed independently, manages its own specific database, and scales independently.\n\n*Tradeoffs:* Monoliths are vastly easier to develop, test, and deploy, but become impossible to maintain as they grow huge. Microservices scale infinitely and allow teams to work independently, but introduce massive operational complexity (networking, distributed transactions, tracing).",
    "example": "If a memory leak in the \"Reporting\" module crashes a Monolith, the entire company goes offline. In Microservices, only the Reporting service crashes; Sales and Operations continue fine.",
    "code": "// Monolith Deployment: 1 Server, 64GB RAM\n// Microservices Deployment: 20 Servers, 2GB RAM each",
    "tip": "Martin Fowler famously advises: Don't start with Microservices. Start with a well-structured Monolith, and carve out microservices only when scaling or team size demands it.",
    "mistake": "Assuming microservices are always superior. For a small team building a simple CRUD app, microservices will add crushing, unnecessary infrastructure overhead.",
    "summary": "A monolith deploys all code as a single cohesive unit. Microservices split the code into independent, networked processes."
