    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "The Saga pattern is how you handle **Distributed Transactions** across microservices. Since you cannot use a single ACID database transaction across three different services with three different databases, you break the transaction into a sequence of local transactions.\nIf a local transaction in the sequence fails, the Saga executes a series of **Compensating Transactions** (undo operations) to roll back the previous steps.",
    "example": "Booking a trip. Step 1: Book Flight (Success). Step 2: Book Hotel (Success). Step 3: Book Rental Car (Fails! No cars left). \nBecause Step 3 failed, the Saga pattern automatically triggers \"Cancel Hotel\" and \"Cancel Flight\" endpoints to revert the entire process.",
    "code": "// There is no \"ROLLBACK\" in microservices. You must write explicit UNDO logic.\nif (carFailed) {\n    restTemplate.postForObject(\"http://hotel/cancel\", hotelBookingId);\n    restTemplate.postForObject(\"http://flight/cancel\", flightBookingId);\n}",
    "tip": "Sagas are usually implemented using Asynchronous events (Choreography) or a central orchestrator service (Orchestration).",
    "mistake": "Trying to use traditional Two-Phase Commit (2PC) locks across microservices. It causes massive performance bottlenecks.",
    "summary": "A pattern for distributed transactions that executes a sequence of local transactions, using specific 'undo' (compensating) transactions to rollback if a step fails."
