    "title": "What is CORS?",
    "category": "10. Spring Security",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a security mechanism enforced by web browsers. \nBy default, a web browser running a frontend application on `http://localhost:3000` (React) will BLOCK any AJAX/fetch requests sent to a backend API running on `http://localhost:8080` (Spring Boot) because they are on different \"Origins\" (domains/ports).\nTo fix this, the backend server must be configured to send specific HTTP headers (like `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`) telling the browser: \"It's okay, I allow requests from localhost:3000.\"",
    "example": "If you don't configure CORS on your Spring Boot backend, your React frontend will show a nasty red `CORS policy blocked` error in the console.",
    "code": "// Fixing CORS globally in Spring Boot\n@Configuration\npublic class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {\n    @Override\n    public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {\n        registry.addMapping(\"/**\")\n                .allowedOrigins(\"http://localhost:3000\")\n                .allowedMethods(\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\");\n    }\n}",
    "tip": "CORS is exclusively a browser security feature. A mobile app or a cURL script from the terminal will never get a CORS error.",
