    "category": "11. Caching & Performance",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "(This is a crucial concept, often asked. Reiterating from the JPA section for performance context).\nThe N+1 problem is a performance flaw where fetching a list of N entities, and then accessing their lazy child associations, results in N+1 total database queries instead of 1.\n**Solution:** You solve it by explicitly telling the ORM to fetch the parent and the children together in a single query using a SQL JOIN.",
    "example": "Loading 50 Authors and their Books results in 1 query for Authors, and 50 queries for Books. Total 51 queries. A `JOIN FETCH` reduces this to 1 single query.",
    "code": "// Instead of relying on lazy loading, force a single query\n@Query(\"SELECT a FROM Author a JOIN FETCH a.books\")\nList<Author> findAllAuthorsWithBooks();",
