    "title": "What is an in-memory database (e.g., H2)?",
    "category": "12. Testing Backend Applications",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "An in-memory database is a database management system that primarily relies on main memory (RAM) for computer data storage, rather than a disk drive. \nIn the Java world, H2 is the most famous. It boots up instantly with your Spring application, stores all data in memory, and completely disappears (wiping all data) the second the application shuts down.\nIt is historically used for running fast Integration Tests without needing to install a real database on the developer's laptop.",
    "example": "If you add the H2 dependency to your `pom.xml` and run a `@DataJpaTest`, Spring completely ignores your MySQL config, boots an H2 database, runs your tests, and destroys it in 2 seconds.",
