    "id": 180,
    "title": "How do you test a REST API endpoint?",
    "category": "12. Testing Backend Applications",
    "language": "java",
    "explanation": "To fully test a REST API endpoint in Spring Boot, you use `@WebMvcTest` combined with `MockMvc`.\nYou write a test that verifies four distinct things:\n1. **HTTP Status:** Does it return 200 OK, 201 Created, or 400 Bad Request?\n2. **Headers:** Does it return the correct Content-Type (application/json)?\n3. **Payload Structure/Data:** Does the returned JSON contain the exact fields and values expected?\n4. **Business Logic Delegation:** Did the controller actually pass the correct data down to the mocked Service layer?",
    "example": "Testing a GET endpoint to ensure it returns 200 OK and a JSON array of users.",
    "code": "@Test\npublic void testGetUser() throws Exception {\n    // Setup Mock\n    when(userService.findById(1L)).thenReturn(new User(1L, \"Alice\"));\n    \n    // Execute Request and Assert\n    mockMvc.perform(get(\"/api/users/1\")\n           .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))\n           .andExpect(status().isOk())\n           .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.id\").value(1))\n           .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.name\").value(\"Alice\"));\n}",
    "tip": "Using `jsonPath` is preferred over converting the JSON back to an Object in the test, because it explicitly verifies the exact string structure sent over the wire.",
    "mistake": "Testing the database inside a controller test. The controller test should only care about HTTP routing and JSON serialization.",
    "summary": "By using `MockMvc` to simulate HTTP requests and applying assertions on the resulting HTTP status codes, headers, and JSON body structures."
